  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 203 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThe cold front will progress eastward Thursday evening into \novernight, but some showers could linger behind the front well into \ninto the night along/east of I-44/I-55. The primary impact of the \nfront will be the onset of strong low-level CAA that persists \nthrough the day Friday. In fact, Friday's calendar day high \ntemperature will be at midnight with temperatures falling and then \nwavering in the 40s and 50s F through the day Friday beneath \ninsolation limited by extensive stratocumulus and even isolated \nshowers in the cyclonic flow region beneath the main upper-level \ntrough. Occasional northwesterly winds gusting 25 to 35 mph will \nfurther exacerbate the chill of these much cooler temperatures.\n\nThe seasonable cool airmass will support high temperatures in the \n50s and low-60s F on Saturday and low temperatures in the mid-30s to \nlow-40s F Saturday morning and Sunday morning. Uncertainties in \nlingering cloud cover Friday night and the positioning of a surface \nanticyclone decrease confidence in how widespread frost will be \nthose mornings, dependent on how optimal radiational cooling and BL \ndecoupling are. It is worth noting that global ensemble model and \nNBM-based probabilities of low temperatures below 36 F, a reasonable \nproxy for initial frost formation, have been slowly increasing for \nlow-lying and rural areas.\n\nGlobal model guidance is in good agreement that a warming trend back \nto above average will take place Sunday through early next week as \nthe anticyclone migrates to the east and weak but persistent low-\nlevel WAA begins. In the southwesterly upper-level flow pattern, \nmodel guidance is having a more difficult time resolving a potential \npassing upper-level trough that only a small amount (<15 percent) of \nensemble membership has producing light showers in minimal moisture \nreturn. Therefore, dry conditions are more than likely to prevail \nthrough the weekend and early next week.",
